Engine-boundary restoration through pipeline state and StateCache
================================================================

Source proposal: docs/Rendering.EngineBoundaryRestoration.Proposal.md
Approval: user approved the proposal on 2026-10-05. Phases 1–6 completed; acceptance consolidation and supplied live validation remain pending.
Architecture: docs/Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.Proposal.md
Parent plan: docs/Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.todo / Approved sequencing exception.
Project index: project.todo / Issues / Current / legacy fixed-function helper retirement.

Goal and execution contract
  Restore interrupted engine state through the existing StateCache/PSO transition machinery,
  correcting refraction capture without adding manual state management to VGE renderers.
  The source proposal is authoritative; this plan sequences its bounded implementation.
  Keep all new tasks unchecked until their complete requirements and verification are satisfied.
  Delegate every build and test run to subagents. Never launch Vintage Story.
  Record source, unit, headless integration, supplied live evidence and measured costs separately.
  Reuse the parent plan's implementation evidence document, docs/Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.md
  (create during implementation if absent), with a distinct engine-boundary restoration section.
  Follow XML comments, visibility regions and nontrivial-method comments; use responsibility-based
  names without new Gl/GL/Gpu prefixes. Keep boundary composition thin and files responsibility-focused.

Scope and exclusions
  Bring forward only the shared restoration mechanism, necessary state coverage and refraction
  integration under the approved sequencing exception. Do not claim completion of the parent plan.
  Categorize all existing fixed-function tracking values into data structs as a foundation for
  snapshots and future PSOs; keep cache knowledge separate and resource-binding caches unchanged.
  VGE passes use PSO application or declared dynamic state; native setters are backend/integration APIs.
  Preserve existing shader preparation/input publication, engine-aware activation, resource ownership,
  framebuffer binding scopes and deferred resource retirement. Do not create duplicate implementations.
  No renderer-specific restore PSO, second native-state cache, hand-coded renderer restore sequence,
  complete-pipeline/submission implementation, render graph or new resource hierarchy.
  No nested engine boundaries or nested graphics passes. Sequential VGE passes share one boundary.
  Engine hooks cover supported paths, not arbitrary mods/raw GL. Retain justified external fallbacks.
  Refraction UV fallback, ray algorithms, aesthetic fading and the separate bottom-cutoff diagnosis
  are out of scope. Existing RenderDoc evidence proves the defect, not a future fix.

Phase 1: Establish boundary coverage and implementation contracts
  Dependencies: approved source proposal and parent-plan sequencing exception.
  Outcome: a complete, reviewable contract for the first boundary and all affected entry points.
  Completed 2026-10-05 after second source review and independent audit-stage-completion review.
  Evidence: docs/Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.md / Engine-boundary restoration.
  Source and installed-IL validation only; no runtime changes, build/test run or game launch.

  ✔ Inventory the pre-overlay hook and full WaterRefractionCapture.Capture call chain.
    ✔ Trace direct lighting, composite, allocation/resize, target setup, clears, draw helpers,
      shader activation and cleanup; include exception and early-return paths.
    ✔ Trace installed engine callers and resumed first-person draws; distinguish established
      incoming state from values the engine actually resets after the interruption.
    ✔ Inventory ordinary registered direct-lighting/composite callbacks separately from capture.
    ✔ Classify each fixed-function, dynamic and binding effect as observed, explicitly preserved,
      reestablished by a verified engine contract, or excluded from the supported boundary.
  ✔ Inventory every CaptureLegacyFixedFunctionState caller before proposing its disposition.
    ✔ Identify independent engine boundaries, work inside an existing boundary and redundant scopes.
    ✔ Record necessary retained callers and their migration dependencies; do not blanket-delete them.
  ✔ Resolve the shared boundary API and source layout within existing owners.
    ✔ Map every current fixed-function field across StateCache partial files to exactly one category,
      including patch size and provoking convention, plus the viewport coverage introduced here.
    ✔ Specify category value structs and separate per-field/per-output validity metadata; retain
      partial knowledge without treating unknown values as PSO settings or native defaults.
    ✔ Settle category placement and static/dynamic policy against the approved PSO architecture;
      resource-binding caches and unsupported future state are outside this storage refactor.
    ✔ Define coverage metadata derived from PSO/dynamic declarations plus documented helper effects;
      exclude duplicate renderer-maintained pipeline value lists.
    ✔ Define the effective preservation set, including global/indexed aliases and all setup/cleanup effects.
    ✔ Select a concrete context identity/generation source and loss/replacement recovery contract.
    ✔ Specify supported dynamic state, viewport overloads and any required indexed write-mask coverage.
    ✔ Define entry failure versus restoration failure handling, including propagation through capture's
      existing optional-failure catch; never report a failed restore as safe unchanged continuation.
    ✔ Define non-nesting, exactly-once disposal and cleanup ordering with existing binding/shader scopes.
  ✔ Record the coverage matrix, decisions, source links and deterministic reference cases in the
    parent implementation evidence document. Map every proposal section to this plan's work.
  ✔ Completion gate: coverage includes every affected entry point and helper; no unresolved context,
    failure-policy or state-ownership choice blocks foundational implementation.

Phase 2: Categorize state storage and establish cache-backed coverage
  Dependencies: Phase 1 inventory and state/context decisions.
  Outcome: categorized fixed-function storage supports truthful cache-owned transitions before snapshots.
  Completed 2026-10-05 after second source review and audit-stage-completion reconciliation.
  Evidence: docs/Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.md / Categorized storage and cache-backed coverage.
  Delegated Release/Debug builds passed; 59 focused tests passed with no failures or skips.

  ✔ Replace loose fixed-function tracking fields with the inventoried category structs.
    ✔ Introduce focused concrete value types for depth, output blending/write masks, rasterization,
      primitive assembly and dynamic state as established in Phase 1; one responsibility per file.
    ✔ Maintain separate cache-validity metadata at field/index granularity; preserve partially known state.
    ✔ Migrate existing setters, readers, engine adapters and selective invalidation together; remove
      duplicate authoritative fields rather than maintaining a second cache during normal operation.
    ✔ Keep native queries/transitions, alias handling and restoration in StateCache; value structs
      must not issue GL calls or own independent state caches.
    ✔ Reuse existing value types where suitable; expose reusable category values for snapshots and
      future PSO composition without requiring the complete future descriptor implementation.
    ✔ Define independent ownership/copying for indexed payloads; a struct containing an array must
      not let snapshots or published descriptors alias mutable cache storage.
    ✔ Preserve resource-binding caches, VAO associations, resource retirement and native-name reuse rules.
  ✔ Delegate storage-refactor regression checks before boundary snapshots depend on the new layout.
    ✔ Verify full field-to-category coverage, independent field/index validity and selective invalidation.
    ✔ Verify existing transition behavior/call suppression and unaffected resource-binding lifetimes.
  ✔ Extend the existing StateCache application backend only for inventoried missing coverage.
    ✔ Model blend enables/factors per draw-output index; retain a uniform fast path only when proven.
    ✔ Global mutations update or invalidate every overlapping indexed entry; indexed mutations
      withdraw any assertion that all indices have the same global value.
    ✔ Use the context's cached supported draw-buffer limit; preserve indices beyond the temporary
      target's routing and distinguish draw-output indices from attachment numbers.
    ✔ Apply equivalent alias handling to global/indexed write masks and other affected operations.
      Do not flatten unsupported incoming state; expand required coverage or reject before mutation.
    ✔ Preserve unrelated cache knowledge and existing resource-specific lifetime handling.
  ✔ Add cache-backed viewport application and required engine-call observation.
    ✔ Route GpuFramebuffer.BindWithViewport through the cache owner, retaining compatibility behavior.
    ✔ Express viewport as dynamic state, outside static PSO identity; cover required scissor/dynamic
      operations only where the boundary inventory requires them.
    ✔ Reuse exact-signature engine mappings; avoid duplicate native calls and recursive hooks.
  ✔ Cover inventoried clear-color helper state through StateCache resource-operation storage and
    exact-signature observation, outside static PSO identity. Boundary cleanup uses this same backend;
    resolved capture and restoration of the helper value follow in Phases 3–4.
  ✔ Associate affected state knowledge/capabilities with the selected context identity/generation.
    ✔ Discard obsolete knowledge on loss/replacement/switch according to the verified context contract.
  ✔ Delegate focused transition tests against actual GL state and representative rendered output.
    ✔ Cover global-to-indexed, indexed-to-global and mixed enables/factors/write masks.
    ✔ Cover viewport observation/application, repeated-operation suppression and context changes.
  ✔ Completion gate: actual native state and cache knowledge agree for all affected operations;
    all existing fixed-function values use categorized storage with separate validity and no duplicate
    authority; required coverage exists before preservation scopes or consumers rely on it.

Phase 3: Implement declared boundary entry and resolved snapshots
  Dependencies: Phases 1–2.
  Outcome: the boundary can establish its restoration contract before optional VGE work starts.
  Completed 2026-10-05 after second source review and independent audit-stage-completion reconciliation.
  Evidence: docs/Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.md / Declared boundary entry and resolved snapshots.
  Delegated Release/Debug builds passed; 76 focused tests passed with no failures or skips.
  This phase established the entry token; Phase 4 below adds its disposable restoration contract.

  ✔ Add focused boundary declarations and internal resolved-state snapshots within the chosen owners.
    ✔ Derive pipeline coverage from descriptors/dynamic declarations and union declared helper effects.
    ✔ Expand global-operation coverage to every native field/index that the operation can change.
    ✔ Keep resolved incoming values separate from authored PSOs, pipeline keys and shader/resource identity.
    ✔ Compose snapshots from categorized concrete values and explicit coverage; require resolution of
      every covered field and retain no mutable alias to live indexed cache payloads.
  ✔ Resolve incoming coverage at entry using valid cached values and queries only for missing values.
    ✔ Complete resolution before drawing-state mutation; use known-state contracts only with evidence.
    ✔ Failed queries do not substitute defaults or publish a usable scope; entry failure prevents
      the optional operation and leaves its publication unavailable and native state unchanged.
    ✔ Retain the snapshot independently of later cache invalidation and bind it to its context generation.
  ✔ Validate covered managed mutations before native operations, including backend/integration entry points.
    ✔ Reject out-of-coverage operations before mutation; require contract expansion before execution.
    ✔ Reject nested boundaries and avoid introducing query work inside steady-state managed draws.
  ✔ Delegate tests for known/unknown/partially invalidated entry state, alias coverage, failed queries,
    undeclared mutations, snapshot independence and nested-entry rejection.
    ✔ Mutate indexed cache payloads after capture and verify saved values remain unchanged; unknown
      uncaptured fields must not become implicitly captured defaults through category aggregation.
  ✔ Completion gate: entry either produces a complete context-bound snapshot or makes no drawing-state
    change; all scoped mutation paths enforce the declared coverage.

Phase 4: Implement restoration, cleanup and failure guarantees
  Dependencies: Phase 3 snapshots and Phase 2 transition backend.
  Outcome: success and supported failure paths return required native state with truthful cache knowledge.
  Completed 2026-10-05 after second source review and independent audit-stage-completion reconciliation.
  Evidence: docs/Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.md / Scoped restoration, borrowed bindings and failure guarantees.
  Delegated Release/Debug builds passed; 105 focused tests passed with no failures or skips.
  Production refraction/lighting/composite migration remains Phase 5; no live fix claim.

  ✔ Restore the effective preservation set through the existing cached transition backend.
    ✔ Compare against known current state and suppress redundant calls; restore saved values even
      when current knowledge is invalidated, without erasing the incoming snapshot or inventing defaults.
    ✔ Preserve mixed indexed values; never restore a global value afterward that overwrites them.
    ✔ Do not use InvalidateAll merely to force restoration; leave unrelated state alone.
  ✔ Implement exception-safe exactly-once cleanup with the declared adapter ordering.
    ✔ Coordinate shader ownership and borrowed bindings before final pipeline restoration where their
      cleanup can mutate covered state; reuse UseScope and independent read/draw framebuffer scopes.
    ✔ Preserve the declared prepared-resource footprint, active texture unit and quad geometry bindings
      through existing binding owners; reject unavailable foreign footprints before optional capture.
    ✔ Reject restoration into a different context generation and follow the selected recovery contract.
    ✔ Surface native restoration failures; mark affected knowledge unknown and apply the documented
      failure policy without hiding either the original operation error or restoration failure.
    ✔ Preserve explicit external-operation boundaries and affected-category invalidation for unknown calls.
  ✔ Delegate tests for no-op and A-to-B-to-A transitions, invalidation during a scope, setup/draw/cleanup
    failures, context mismatch, exactly-once disposal and restoration failure reporting.
    ✔ Assert engine shader ownership and actual native state as well as cache fields.
    ✔ Record query/native-call counts for cold entry, partially known entry and unchanged warm operation.
  ✔ Completion gate: scoped restoration and failure guarantees pass before production capture migration;
    the mechanism requires no renderer-owned state queries or restoration sequences.

Phase 5: Integrate refraction and independent lighting/composite callbacks
  Dependencies: Phase 4 restoration evidence and Phase 1 entry-point contracts.
  Outcome: the complete pre-overlay interruption uses one proven engine boundary.
  Completed 2026-10-05 after second source review and independent audit-stage-completion review.
  Evidence: docs/Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.md / Refraction and fullscreen callback integration.
  Delegated Release/Debug builds passed; 213 distinct tests passed with no failures or skips.
  Headless mixed-index correction verified; live appearance and separate UV cutoff remain pending.

  ✔ Place the boundary adapter around capture setup, direct lighting, composite and cleanup.
    ✔ Remove redundant outer CapturePipeline setup after confirming each pass establishes its requirements.
    ✔ Remove intermediate legacy snapshots from the shared capture work; retain distinct binding and
      shader scopes that the contract still needs. Reject accidental nested engine boundaries.
    ✔ Adapt independently registered direct-lighting/composite callbacks to their own required boundaries.
      Include composite preparation, SSAO restoration and receiver reduction in its declared coverage.
    ✔ Keep orchestration thin; isolate boundary policy from resource allocation and shading algorithms.
  ✔ Preserve refraction publication, failure, resize, reload, toggle and resource lifetime behavior.
    ✔ Failed optional entry leaves capture unpublished and does not interrupt the original engine draw.
    ✔ Keep restoration-failure handling distinct from recoverable capture failure.
    ✔ Review each local blanket invalidation against tracked coverage; remove only proven redundancy.
  ✔ Delegate the regression reproducing the supplied capture's mixed indexed state.
    ✔ Enter with engine-like depth state, blend enabled for outputs 0–2 and disabled for metadata.
    ✔ Execute actual capture then real first-person marker-writing draws; verify negative markers survive.
    ✔ Verify final refraction publication restores the coherent world color/depth pair behind the overlay.
  ✔ Delegate ordinary/isolated lighting and composite regressions for output equivalence, viewport,
    independent read/draw bindings, first use, repeated frames, resize, reload, failures and disabled refraction.
  ✔ Completion gate: capture and independent callbacks preserve their verified engine contracts and
    resource ownership; headless evidence reproduces the old defect and verifies its correction.

Phase 6: Review legacy callers and reconcile future PSO adoption
  Dependencies: Phase 5 integration evidence and Phase 1 caller inventory.
  Outcome: each legacy caller has an evidenced disposition and the shared mechanism has one future owner.

  ✔ Reconcile every inventoried legacy caller against the proven mechanism.
    ✔ Migrate only callers with established boundary coverage and focused validation; remove scopes
      proven redundant inside an existing boundary or unnecessary for their actual mutations.
    ✔ Retain necessary unconverted callers with explicit reasons and linked parent-plan prerequisites;
      do not imply that the immediate refraction correction converts every graphics consumer.
    ✔ Retire the legacy helper only when no necessary caller remains; otherwise retain a tracked removal task.
  ✔ Perform a second source review against the approved proposal, actual entry points and test evidence.
    ✔ Check one owner per state policy, cleanup ordering, unknown mutation handling and PSO-only VGE state.
    ✔ Confirm the category inventory is complete, knowledge stays separate from reusable values,
      and no resource-binding ownership or lifetime rules moved into fixed-function structs.
    ✔ Resolve confirmed discrepancies before marking migration work complete.
  ✔ Update proposal/parent-plan/project links and implementation evidence without checking unrelated work.
    ✔ Record how GraphicsCommandContext will consume the boundary mechanism during complete submission.
    ✔ Assign renderer compatibility-wiring removal to the corresponding parent consumer migrations.
    ✔ Retain partial overrides only at declared compatibility boundaries; keep viewport dynamic and
      framebuffer blend-policy migration with the parent plan's complete-pipeline consumers.
  ✔ Completion gate: no caller or temporary path has an unexplained disposition; retained migration work
    is traceable and the bounded sequencing exception does not weaken broader completion requirements.

Phase 7: Consolidate acceptance evidence and supplied live validation
  Dependencies: Phases 1–6.
  Outcome: correctness, implementation scope and live status are reported without overstated claims.

  ☐ Reconcile each proposal section with implemented source, verification or an explicitly justified
    retained parent-plan dependency. No unresolved in-scope defect may be labeled complete.
  ☐ Delegate affected builds and focused unit/headless suites using repository configurations.
    ☐ Reuse valid focused receipts; rerun checks affected by subsequent changes and report failures/skips.
    ☐ Include shared cache, engine mapping, shader ownership and unaffected-binding regression coverage.
  ☐ Consolidate measured boundary query/native-transition counts and distinguish them from performance
    expectations; make no unmeasured CPU/GPU improvement claim.
  ☐ Obtain user-supplied live observations or a new RenderDoc capture without launching the game.
    ☐ Confirm first-person appearance, indexed metadata state and refraction-source cleanliness.
    ☐ Record remaining bottom-cutoff behavior separately; do not treat this plan as a UV-fallback fix.
    ☐ If evidence is unavailable, report automated completion separately and leave live acceptance pending.
  ☐ Final acceptance: required implementation/review/tests pass, caller dispositions and parent links
    are current, supplied live acceptance is recorded, and no scope/performance claim exceeds evidence.

Open design choices and assumptions
  API spellings in the proposal are illustrative; Phase 1 selects concrete names and layout.
  Category names and validity-metadata representation are design choices; categorized structs,
  separate field/index knowledge and StateCache's single transition ownership are requirements.
  Context identity/generation and recovery must use an established owner where available; existing
  thread-local access alone does not prove context identity. Resolve this before snapshots are usable.
  The precise helper/resource-binding effects and required indexed mask/dynamic coverage come from
  inventory, not from assuming that all state is already observed by engine hooks.
  Restoration failure behavior must be chosen explicitly; optional-capture fallback is valid for failed
  entry, not a guarantee after failed cleanup. Existing broad catch paths need review accordingly.
  Known-state entry contracts and broader legacy-caller migration require individual evidence.
  User-supplied live evidence may remain pending after automated checks; it is not replaced by them.
